{
  "_comment": "Canonical task-status registry -- the single source of truth for every consumer that needs to know what a task's top-level `status:` field can legally be, which stage/column it belongs to, whether it's terminal, its legal next action, and which task artifact file becomes relevant at that point. Ships to <project>/.claude/task-status-registry.json (always synced via init/upgrade/drift/remove -- reference data, never user-owned). Consumers: templates/scripts/check-overview.py (legality + terminal), templates/scripts/caw-status.sh (terminal for its Active/Finished split, via a python3 one-liner -- next_action/artifact are recorded here for completeness and cross-checked by scripts/tests/status-registry-parity.sh against caw-status.sh's own next_cmd() output, but caw-status.sh's bash case statement remains the executable implementation, not a generated one, since it already handles the next_phase-dependent dynamic parts these two fields can't express statically), tools/backlog-viewer/scripts/generate-status-map.mjs (stage/label, generates src/lib/status-map.generated.ts at build time). next_action is one of: plan|code|verify|review|commit|unblock|status|none (status = no single next step, matches caw-status.sh's generic */fallback case; unblock = human must unblock first). artifact is the task file (plan.md/code.md/tests.md/review.md) that stage's own agent writes, or null for statuses with no single relevant artifact (pending, blocked, terminal). Phase-level status (phases[].status) is a separate, smaller enum documented in conductor/templates/task-plan-reference.md and validated by check-overview.py's own PHASE_STATUS constant -- not covered here, no cross-consumer drift was found for it.",
